const t = (title, theory, example, question, correct, distractors, reason) => ({title,theory,example,quiz:{question,correct,distractors,reason}});
const s = (id,name,topics,matches) => ({id,name,topics,matches});

export const curriculum = [
s('network','Network Theory',[
t('Voltage, current, charge, power, energy and Ohm’s law','Voltage is energy per unit charge. Current is the rate at which charge passes a point, i = dq/dt. A linear resistor obeys v = iR. With current entering the positive-voltage terminal, p = vi is power absorbed and electrical energy is the integral of power over time.','A 6 Ω resistor with 2 A through it drops 12 V and absorbs 24 W. If held for 5 s it absorbs 120 J.','A 4 Ω resistor carries 3 A. What power does it absorb?','36 W',['12 W','9 W','48 W'],'Use i²R, which gives 3² × 4 = 36 W.'),
t('Elements, sources and source transformation','An ideal voltage source fixes terminal voltage regardless of load and has zero series resistance. An ideal current source fixes current and has infinite parallel resistance. A practical source adds a series or parallel resistance, respectively. A dependent source takes its value from another circuit variable and remains active during superposition. To transform between practical forms, use Iₛ=Vₛ/R with the same terminal resistance R and check the direction of terminal current.','Turn a 12 V source in series with 3 Ω into a 4 A source in parallel with 3 Ω. With a 6 Ω load, the current divider gives 4·3/(3+6)=4/3 A, matching 12/(3+6).','What current source is equivalent to 15 V in series with 5 Ω?','3 A in parallel with 5 Ω',['3 A in series with 5 Ω','75 A in parallel with 5 Ω','5 A in parallel with 3 Ω'],'Divide the source voltage by the series resistance and place the same resistance in parallel.'),
t('Series, parallel, voltage divider, current divider and star–delta','Series resistors carry the same current and divide voltage in proportion to resistance. Parallel branches share voltage and divide current in proportion to conductance. A divider formula applies to the actual circuit, so replace any loaded leg by its loaded equivalent first. For a delta with sides Rₐᵦ, Rᵦ꜀ and R꜀ₐ, the star arm at a is RₐᵦR꜀ₐ divided by the sum of the three sides. In reverse, the delta leg across a and b is Rₐ+Rᵦ+RₐRᵦ/R꜀.','A 12 V divider has a 2 kΩ top leg and 4 kΩ bottom leg. Connect a 4 kΩ load across the output. The bottom equivalent is 2 kΩ, so the loaded output is 12·2/(2+2)=6 V.','A delta has three 9 Ω arms. What is each equivalent star resistance?','3 Ω',['9 Ω','27 Ω','6 Ω'],'Each star arm is 9 × 9 divided by 27.'),
t('Nodes, branches, loops, meshes, KCL and KVL','A branch connects two nodes and carries one branch current. A loop is any closed path, and a mesh is a loop containing no smaller loop in a planar drawing. KCL follows from charge conservation: the signed currents at a node sum to zero. KVL states that signed voltage changes around a lumped circuit loop sum to zero. Choose current directions and loop traversal before writing equations. A negative answer reverses the assumed reference.','A 6 V source feeds two series resistors of 1 Ω and 2 Ω. KCL gives the same current through both resistors. Traversing from the source rise through both drops gives 6−I−2I=0, so I=2 A and the drops are 2 V and 4 V.','Which law follows from conservation of charge at a node?','KCL',['KVL','Maximum power transfer','Reciprocity'],'KCL balances the current flow into and out of the node.'),
t('Nodal and mesh analysis, supernodes and supermeshes','For nodal analysis, select ground and write KCL using branch currents (V₁−V₂)/R. If a voltage source joins two unknown nodes, enclose both in a supernode and add its voltage constraint. For mesh analysis, assign loop currents to planar meshes and write KVL. A shared resistor carries the difference of the two mesh currents. When a current source is shared, write KVL around the outer supermesh and use the source value to constrain the current difference.','Nodes a and b connect to ground through 2 Ω and 4 Ω, respectively. An ideal source fixes Vₐ−Vᵦ=6 V. Supernode KCL gives Vₐ/2+Vᵦ/4=0. Substituting Vₐ=Vᵦ+6 gives Vᵦ=−4 V and Vₐ=2 V.','An ideal current source lies on the common branch of two meshes. What should you use?','A supermesh and a current constraint',['A supernode and a voltage constraint','Two independent KVL equations through the source','A voltage divider'],'Write KVL around the outer perimeter and use the current source to relate the two mesh currents.'),
t('Superposition, Thévenin, Norton, matching and reciprocity','In a linear network, superposition adds signed voltage or current responses with one independent source active at a time. Set the other ideal voltage sources to shorts and current sources to opens. Keep dependent sources active and use a test source if needed to determine terminal resistance. Thévenin uses the open-circuit voltage and a series resistance. Norton uses the short-circuit current with that resistance in parallel. A resistive load receives maximum power when Rᴸ=Rₜₕ. For AC with adjustable complex impedance, choose the conjugate of Zₜₕ. In reciprocal networks, exchanging excitation and observation ports preserves the corresponding transfer relation.','A two-terminal circuit has Vₒ꜀=12 V and Iₛ꜀=3 A. Its Thévenin resistance is 4 Ω, so a 4 Ω load draws 12/(4+4)=1.5 A and receives 1.5²·4=9 W, the maximum.','When using superposition, how do you turn off an independent ideal voltage source?','Replace it by a short circuit',['Replace it by an open circuit','Keep its original voltage','Replace it by a current source'],'Setting an ideal voltage source to 0 V enforces zero voltage across its terminals.'),
t('Capacitors, inductors, RC, RL and RLC responses','Capacitor current is C dv/dt and inductor voltage is L di/dt. Without an impulse, capacitor voltage and inductor current are continuous at switching. At settled DC, ideal capacitors are open and ideal inductors are short. In a first-order circuit, the state follows final value plus initial deviation times e⁻ᵗ/τ. Use τ=RₑqC or L/Rₑq with resistance seen by the storage element after switching. A series RLC has characteristic polynomial s²+(R/L)s+1/(LC). Its roots determine whether the natural response rings or decays without oscillation. The forced response follows from the applied input.','A 10 μF capacitor initially at 2 V connects through 10 kΩ to a 12 V source. The time constant is 0.1 s. Its voltage is 12+(2−12)e⁻ᵗ/⁰·¹, so after 0.1 s it reaches 12−10/e≈8.32 V.','Which quantity normally cannot jump instantly in an ideal inductor?','Inductor current',['Inductor voltage','Resistor current','Source voltage'],'A finite voltage produces a finite current slope, di/dt = v/L.'),
t('AC phasors, impedance, admittance, RMS and power','Phasors encode the magnitude and phase of sinusoids at one common frequency. In phasor equations, R, jωL and 1/(jωC) replace the element laws. Combine series impedances directly and parallel admittances Y=1/Z directly. RMS amplitude for a sine is peak divided by √2. With RMS phasors, complex power S=VI* has real part P in watts and imaginary part Q in vars. The magnitude |S| is apparent power, and P/|S| is the power factor. Positive Q indicates a net inductive load.','Connect 100 V RMS to Z=6+j8 Ω. The current is 10∠−53.13° A. Then S=1000∠53.13°=600+j800 VA, giving a 0.6 lagging power factor.','A sinusoid has a peak voltage of 10 V. What is its RMS voltage?','About 7.07 V',['5 V','10 V','About 14.14 V'],'Divide the peak by √2 for a sine wave.'),
t('Series and parallel resonance, Q and half-power bandwidth','In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs when ωL=1/(ωC), so ω₀=1/√(LC) and current peaks at V/R. Half-power occurs where |I| falls to its peak divided by √2, equivalently |ωL−1/(ωC)|=R. The bandwidth in radians per second is R/L, and Q=ω₀ divided by that bandwidth. An ideal parallel RLC resonates when net susceptance vanishes and the input impedance peaks. Its Q depends on the parallel loss resistance.','For a series circuit with R=10 Ω, L=0.1 H and C=10 μF, ω₀=1000 rad/s and bandwidth=R/L=100 rad/s. Hence Q=10. At the half-power points the net reactance is ±10 Ω.','At series resonance, what is true of the input impedance of a series RLC?','It is real and minimum',['It is infinite','It equals jωL','It is purely capacitive'],'The L and C reactances cancel, leaving only the series resistance.'),
t('Mutual inductance, dots and two-port parameters','A two-port model relates the two terminal voltages and currents without exposing the internal circuit. With both currents entering, Z maps currents to voltages and Y maps voltages to currents. Open the other port for Z tests and short it for Y tests. Hybrid h maps I₁ and V₂ to V₁ and I₂. ABCD maps V₂ and −I₂ to V₁ and I₁, so cascade matrices multiply in physical order. For coupled coils with voltage references positive at the dots and currents entering both dots, the mutual terms are +M di/dt. Reversing a single reference changes their sign.','For a series resistor R=5 Ω used as a two-port, the through current equals −I₂ and V₁=V₂+5(−I₂). Its ABCD matrix is [[1,5],[0,1]]. Cascading a second identical block gives [[1,10],[0,1]], equivalent to 10 Ω in series.','How is Z11 measured?','With port 2 open',['With port 2 shorted','With port 1 open','With equal port voltages'],'Z parameters use currents as independent variables, so set I2 to zero.')
],[['KCL','Charge balance at a node'],['Supermesh','Shared current source between two meshes'],['Norton model','Current source with parallel impedance'],['ABCD parameters','Matrix multiplication for cascaded two-ports']]),

s('devices','Electronic Devices',[
t('Energy bands and material classes','In a crystal, closely spaced atomic energy levels form bands. The valence band contains bonding states and the conduction band contains states available for mobile electrons. The energy gap Eᵍ separates them. In a direct-gap material, the band edges occur at the same crystal momentum, so a photon can mediate the transition efficiently. Indirect-gap transitions also require a change in momentum, often supplied by a phonon.','For an ideal direct gap of 1.9 eV, a transition at the band edge emits near λ=1240/1.9≈653 nm.','What does band gap mean?','Energy difference between valence-band top and conduction-band bottom',['Average energy of all electrons','Voltage across the sample','Distance between two atoms'],'The gap is an energy interval with no allowed electron states in the ideal crystal.'),
t('Silicon, germanium, intrinsic and doped materials','Thermal excitation creates electron–hole pairs. At equilibrium in intrinsic material n=p=nᵢ. Donors raise n and move the Fermi level toward the conduction band, while acceptors raise p and move it toward the valence band. In the nondegenerate equilibrium approximation np=nᵢ². Determine majority density from charge neutrality and minority density from this product law.','For fully ionized donors Nᴅ=10¹⁶ cm⁻³ and nᵢ=10¹⁰ cm⁻³ with negligible compensation, n≈10¹⁶ cm⁻³ and p≈10⁴ cm⁻³.','Which material has the larger intrinsic carrier concentration at room temperature?','Germanium',['Silicon','Both are identical','It is zero for both'],'Germanium’s smaller band gap makes thermal carrier generation more likely.'),
t('Carriers, Fermi level, mobility, drift and diffusion','An electric field drives drift, with current density q(nμₙ+pμₚ)E. Carrier concentration gradients drive diffusion, with Jₙ=qDₙ dn/dx and Jₚ=−qDₚ dp/dx in one dimension. The Einstein relation links D/μ to kT/q near equilibrium. The continuity equation accounts for current flow, generation and recombination. Poisson’s equation relates potential curvature to space charge.','If n=10¹⁶ cm⁻³, μₙ=1000 cm²/(V·s) and E=10 V/cm, the electron drift current density is 1.6×10⁻¹⁹·10¹⁶·1000·10=16 A/cm².','What drives diffusion?','A carrier concentration gradient',['Only an electric field','Only an applied magnetic field','A constant carrier concentration'],'Random motion produces net movement from high concentration to low concentration.'),
t('PN junction, depletion region, bias and diode equation','Diffusion across a PN junction exposes fixed donor and acceptor ions and creates the depletion field. At equilibrium this field balances diffusion. Forward bias lowers the barrier and increases minority-carrier injection. Reverse bias raises the barrier and widens the depletion region. Before breakdown, the ideal current law is I=Iₛ[eⱽ/⁽ηⱽᵀ⁾−1], where Vᵀ=kT/q and η is the ideality factor.','At room temperature with Vᵀ≈26 mV and η=1, raising a clearly forward-biased junction by 60 mV multiplies current by e^(60/26)≈10.','What does forward bias do to the junction barrier?','Reduces it',['Increases it','Leaves it unchanged','Reverses the doping types'],'Forward bias opposes the built-in junction field and lets more carriers cross.'),
t('Leakage, breakdown and diode capacitances','Reverse current before breakdown is small and depends on minority carriers and generation in the depletion region. Heavy doping and a narrow depletion region favor Zener tunneling. At higher fields across wider junctions, impact ionization produces avalanche multiplication. Junction capacitance comes from depletion charge and falls as reverse voltage widens the depletion layer. Forward bias can add significant diffusion capacitance from stored carriers.','If an abrupt junction has Cⱼ₀=12 pF and built-in potential 0.75 V, the depletion approximation gives Cⱼ=12/√(1+2.25/0.75)=6 pF at 2.25 V reverse bias.','What dominates avalanche breakdown?','Impact ionization',['Quantum tunneling alone','Ohmic conduction','Radiative recombination'],'Carriers gain energy and generate additional electron–hole pairs by collisions.'),
t('Zener, LED, photodiode, solar cell, Schottky and varactor','A Zener diode shunts current in reverse breakdown to hold a nearly fixed voltage, with a series resistor limiting current. An LED uses radiative recombination under forward bias. In a photodiode, absorbed photons generate carriers and the junction field separates them. Reverse bias widens the collection region. A solar cell uses the photovoltaic effect to supply power to a load and has distinct open-circuit voltage and short-circuit current.','A photodiode with responsivity 0.5 A/W receives 20 μW. Generated photocurrent is 0.5·20 μW=10 μA. If its dark reverse current is 1 μA, the total reverse current magnitude is 11 μA.','Which diode provides voltage-controlled capacitance?','Varactor',['LED','Schottky rectifier','Solar cell'],'The varactor’s reverse bias changes depletion width and therefore capacitance.'),
t('BJTs, operating regions, α/β and CB/CE/CC configurations','In forward active operation, a BJT has a forward-biased emitter junction and reverse-biased collector junction. The collector current is approximately βIᴮ, with Iᴱ=Iᶜ+Iᴮ and α=β/(β+1). Both junctions are forward biased in saturation. In cutoff the base drive is too small to sustain appreciable collector current. Use the external load line to check whether the active-region prediction is physically possible.','A 5 V supply drives a collector through 1 kΩ, with emitter grounded. β=100 and Iᴮ=80 μA predict 8 mA in active mode. The circuit can supply only about (5−0.2)/1000=4.8 mA in saturation, so the transistor saturates.','In forward active operation, β = 100 and IB = 20 µA. What is IC?','2 mA',['20 mA','0.2 mA','100 µA'],'Multiply the base current by the current gain β.'),
t('JFET, MOS capacitor and MOSFET operating regions','A MOS capacitor passes through accumulation, depletion and inversion as gate voltage changes. In an enhancement NMOS, a channel forms above Vᵀ. With overdrive Vᴏᵥ=Vɢs−Vᵀ, the long-channel device operates in triode for 0≤Vᴅs<Vᴏᵥ and in saturation for Vᴅs≥Vᴏᵥ. The ideal saturation current is ½μₙCₒₓ(W/L)Vᴏᵥ². PMOS uses opposite polarities. Scaling changes dimensions and fields, and short-channel effects limit simple long-channel predictions.','An NMOS with Vᵀ=1 V, Vɢs=2 V and Vᴅs=1.5 V is in saturation because 1.5≥1. With μₙCₒₓ(W/L)=2 mA/V², its ideal current is 1 mA.','Which condition marks the start of long-channel NMOS saturation above threshold?','VDS ≥ VGS − VT',['VGS = 0','VDS < VGS − VT','VDS must equal VT'],'Compare drain-source voltage to the gate overdrive VGS−VT.')
],[['Varactor','Voltage-dependent junction capacitance'],['Drift','Motion driven by electric field'],['Avalanche','Carrier multiplication by impact ionization'],['Common collector','Emitter follower']]),

s('analog','Analog Electronics',[
t('Half-wave, centre-tapped and bridge rectifiers','A half-wave rectifier passes one half-cycle, so ripple repeats at the input frequency. Full-wave circuits reverse the negative half-cycle, doubling ripple frequency. A bridge uses two conducting diodes per half-cycle. A centre-tapped circuit uses one conducting diode and half of the secondary winding per half-cycle. With ideal diodes and a resistive load, average voltage is Vₘ/π for half-wave and 2Vₘ/π for full-wave.','A 12 V RMS secondary feeds a bridge. Peak voltage is 12√2≈17 V. With two 0.7 V drops, output peak is about 15.6 V. On a 50 Hz supply the ripple repeats at 100 Hz.','What is the ripple frequency of a 60 Hz full-wave rectifier?','120 Hz',['30 Hz','60 Hz','240 Hz'],'Each input cycle produces two output pulses.'),
t('Efficiency, ripple, PIV, capacitor filters and Zener regulation','Rectifier efficiency is DC load power divided by AC input power. Ripple factor compares the RMS AC component to the DC component. Peak inverse voltage is the largest reverse voltage a nonconducting diode must withstand. A capacitor charges near each crest and discharges into the load between crests, giving small ripple ΔV≈Iᴸ/(fᵣC). A Zener regulator then requires enough resistor current for both load and Zener conduction.','At 100 Hz ripple frequency with C=1000 μF and Iᴸ=20 mA, the estimated ripple is 0.02/(100·0.001)=0.2 V peak to peak.','What does doubling the reservoir capacitance do to ripple at the same load?','Approximately halves it',['Approximately doubles it','Leaves it unchanged','Doubles ripple frequency'],'The capacitor loses less voltage during the same time between peaks.'),
t('Clippers, clampers and voltage multipliers','A clipper sets a ceiling or floor by making a diode conduct when the input crosses a reference plus the diode drop. A clamper uses a diode and capacitor to shift the waveform’s DC level while preserving peak-to-peak swing when its discharge time is long relative to the period. Check the diode’s state in each part of the cycle to derive the output.','An ideal positive clamper clamps the negative peak of a ±3 V sine to 0 V. The capacitor stores a 3 V shift, giving output from 0 to 6 V.','Which circuit ideally shifts the DC level while preserving peak-to-peak voltage?','Clamper',['Clipper','Comparator','Bridge rectifier'],'A clamp establishes a voltage reference for one waveform extreme.'),
t('Transistor bias, DC load line, Q-point and thermal stability','A DC load line expresses the supply constraint, such as Vᶜᴱ=Vᶜᶜ−IᶜRᶜ for a grounded-emitter stage. The Q-point is its intersection with the device bias characteristic. Include emitter resistance or source resistance when present. This gives local feedback: increasing current raises emitter or source voltage, reducing the controlling junction or gate voltage. Choose a Q-point that allows signal swing before cutoff or saturation.','With Vᶜᶜ=12 V and Rᶜ=2 kΩ, a 3 mA bias gives Vᶜᴱ=6 V with emitter grounded. The ideal load-line endpoints are (0 mA,12 V) and (6 mA,0 V).','What is the Q-point?','The no-signal DC operating point',['The frequency of maximum gain','The maximum reverse voltage','The input at full cutoff only'],'It fixes the operating current and voltages around which small-signal behavior is calculated.'),
t('Small-signal CE/CB/CC and CS/CG/CD amplifiers','Linearize the transistor around its bias point. A BJT has gₘ=Iᶜ/Vᵀ and rπ=β/gₘ. An ideal long-channel saturated MOSFET has gₘ=2Iᴅ/Vᴏᵥ. CE and CS stages invert, with gain near −gₘ times the effective load when degeneration is absent. CC and CD followers provide a noninverting gain below one and low output resistance. CB and CG stages have low input resistance and noninverting voltage gain.','For a bypassed CE stage biased at 1 mA, gₘ≈1 mA/26 mV=38.5 mS. With collector load 2 kΩ and negligible output resistance effects, voltage gain is approximately −77 V/V.','Which MOS configuration is a source follower?','Common drain',['Common source','Common gate','Common body'],'The gate drives the device and the output is taken from the source.'),
t('Frequency response, coupling, bypass and multistage gain','Coupling capacitors block DC but form high-pass sections with the surrounding resistances. A bypass capacitor reduces emitter or source degeneration for AC as its impedance falls. Internal capacitances dominate higher-frequency roll-off, and the Miller effect can magnify a capacitance across an inverting gain stage. For cascaded stages, find the loaded gain of each stage and multiply them.','A 1 μF input coupling capacitor sees 1 kΩ source resistance in series with 9 kΩ input resistance. Its corner is 1/(2π·10 kΩ·1 μF)≈15.9 Hz.','Which effect can increase effective input capacitance in an inverting amplifier?','Miller effect',['Avalanche multiplication','Source transformation','Nyquist sampling'],'A capacitance across an inverting stage sees a large voltage change across its terminals.'),
t('Differential amplifiers, current mirrors and CMRR','A differential pair steers its tail current according to the input voltage difference. Rejecting common-mode input depends on device symmetry and tail-source resistance. CMRR=|Aᴅ/A꜀ᴍ|, often expressed as 20log₁₀ of that ratio. A current mirror uses a diode-connected reference transistor to establish a controlling voltage and copies the current according to device ratios, subject to output compliance.','If Aᴅ=80 and A꜀ᴍ=0.02, CMRR=4000 or about 72 dB. A 0.1 V common-mode input contributes 2 mV at the output.','What should an ideal differential amplifier do to equal signals on both inputs?','Reject them',['Amplify them by twice the differential gain','Convert them to a ramp','Always saturate'],'Equal inputs create zero differential voltage.'),
t('Ideal and practical op-amps, virtual ground and basic stages','An ideal op-amp has very large open-loop gain and draws no input current. Linear operation under negative feedback gives nearly equal input voltages. An inverting stage has gain −Rꜰ/Rᵢ, a noninverting stage has gain 1+Rꜰ/Rɢ, and a follower uses unity feedback. Check supply limits, finite bandwidth and slew rate before using these results in a practical stage.','With Rᵢ=10 kΩ, Rꜰ=40 kΩ and a 0.5 V input, the ideal inverting amplifier gives −2 V. This result requires available output swing beyond −2 V.','What is the gain of an ideal voltage follower?','+1',['−1','0','Infinite'],'Feedback connects the output directly to the inverting input, so the output follows the noninverting input.'),
t('Summer, difference amp, integrator, differentiator and comparators','At an inverting summing node, KCL gives Vₒ=−RꜰΣ(Vᵢ/Rᵢ). Matched ratios in a difference amplifier scale the difference and reject common-mode input. A capacitor in feedback produces integration, while an input capacitor with resistive feedback produces differentiation over the intended band. A comparator switches at a threshold. Positive feedback creates the two thresholds and hysteresis of a Schmitt trigger.','An ideal integrator with R=100 kΩ and C=0.1 μF has RC=0.01 s. A constant +1 V input gives output slope −100 V/s. Starting at zero, the output is −0.5 V after 5 ms.','Which circuit has two switching thresholds for noise immunity?','Schmitt trigger',['Voltage follower','Ideal integrator','Inverting summer'],'Positive feedback introduces hysteresis.'),
t('Negative and positive feedback, gain-bandwidth and Barkhausen','Negative feedback gives closed-loop gain A/(1+Aβ) and trades excess gain for predictable response. Frequency-dependent phase can make negative feedback effectively positive. At unity loop gain, phase margin measures distance from −180°. Dominant-pole compensation rolls off loop gain before higher poles add too much phase lag. A positive-feedback oscillator requires a full-turn loop phase and unity loop magnitude in steady state, with startup gain generally above unity.','A loop crosses unity with phase −125°, so its phase margin is 55°. If a higher crossover moves the phase to −170°, the phase margin falls to 10°.','At steady oscillation, what is the loop-gain magnitude in the Barkhausen condition?','1',['0','0.5','Infinite'],'After each cycle the feedback recreates the same amplitude and phase.'),
t('RC, Wien bridge, Hartley, Colpitts and crystal oscillators','An oscillator uses a frequency-selective network and an amplifier. The phase condition selects oscillation frequency, and the loop gain sets startup. A Wien bridge has zero phase and attenuation 1/3 at f₀=1/(2πRC) for equal parts. An RC phase-shift oscillator combines amplifier inversion with an RC phase shift. Amplitude stabilization reduces startup loop gain toward one after oscillation builds.','In a Wien oscillator with R=10 kΩ and C=10 nF, f₀≈1.59 kHz. Set the noninverting amplifier gain near 3, with a small startup margin and amplitude control.','Which oscillator uses a capacitive voltage divider?','Colpitts',['Hartley','Wien bridge only','RC phase-shift'],'The feedback divider in a Colpitts tank consists of two capacitors.'),
t('Power amplifier classes, active filters and 555 timer','An active filter uses an amplifier and reactive components to set poles and zeros. A low-pass passes DC and attenuates high frequencies, a high-pass blocks DC, and band-pass or band-stop responses act around a frequency range. A first-order RC section has f꜀=1/(2πRC) and a 20 dB/decade asymptotic slope. Higher-order sections introduce additional poles and can shape transition steepness and peaking.','A unity-gain buffered low-pass with R=10 kΩ and C=10 nF has f꜀≈1.59 kHz. At f꜀ the magnitude is 1/√2, so a 2 V input amplitude yields about 1.41 V.','Which 555 mode repeatedly oscillates without an external trigger?','Astable',['Monostable','Comparator-only','One-shot'],'The timing capacitor charges and discharges repeatedly between the internal thresholds.')
],[['CMRR','Differential gain divided by common-mode gain'],['Clamper','Moves the waveform DC level'],['Hartley','Inductive feedback divider'],['Schmitt trigger','Uses hysteresis']]),

s('digital','Digital Electronics',[
t('Binary, octal, decimal and hexadecimal','A positional number uses powers of its base. Convert a binary integer by summing the weights where bits are one. Octal and hex correspond to groups of three and four binary bits. Fractional places use negative powers. Binary floating point represents a sign, significand and exponent, trading uniform absolute spacing for a wider range.','1011.01₂=8+2+1+1/4=11.25₁₀. Grouping four bits on each side gives B.4₁₆.','What is 1101₂ in decimal?','13',['11','15','19'],'The place values contribute 8+4+0+1.'),
t('Binary arithmetic, signed numbers, complements and overflow','An N-bit two’s-complement integer ranges from −2ᴺ⁻¹ to 2ᴺ⁻¹−1. Negate by inverting all bits and adding one. Addition keeps the low N bits. Signed overflow occurs when equal-sign inputs generate an opposite-sign result. One’s complement negates by inversion and has two encodings for zero.','Four-bit 0111 represents 7. Adding 0011 gives 1010, interpreted as −6. The true result 10 exceeds the signed maximum 7, so this is overflow.','What range can four-bit two’s complement represent?','−8 to +7',['−7 to +8','−8 to +8','0 to 15'],'One bit pattern represents zero, and the negative side has one additional value.'),
t('BCD, Gray code and parity','BCD encodes each decimal digit independently in four bits. Gray code orders adjacent values so one bit changes per step and is formed by XORing a binary word with itself shifted right by one. An even parity bit makes the overall XOR zero. It can detect an odd number of bit flips.','Decimal 29 is 0010 1001 in BCD. For a four-bit binary value 1010, Gray code is 1010 XOR 0101=1111. The original binary word has two ones and needs an even parity bit of zero.','Which code changes one bit between neighboring count values?','Gray code',['BCD','Ordinary binary in every transition','ASCII'],'Adjacent Gray code words are designed to differ in exactly one position.'),
t('Logic gates, truth tables and universal gates','Evaluate a gate by applying its Boolean rule to every input row. NAND and NOR each form a complete basis because tying their inputs creates an inverter and combinations reproduce AND and OR. In static CMOS, the pull-down NMOS network conducts when output should be low and the complementary PMOS pull-up conducts when output should be high.','In a two-input CMOS NAND, two series NMOS transistors pull down only for A=B=1. Two parallel PMOS transistors pull up if either input is zero, giving output 1 in the other three cases.','Which gate alone can implement any Boolean function?','NAND',['XOR','AND','OR'],'NAND can implement the basic gate set, including inversion.'),
t('Boolean algebra, SOP/POS, minterms, maxterms and K-maps','A minterm is one truth-table row with output one, and SOP adds such terms. A POS expression groups zero rows through maxterms. De Morgan’s laws swap AND and OR under complementation. A Karnaugh map uses Gray ordering so neighboring cells differ in one variable. Group powers of two and keep variables that stay constant within each group. Use don’t-cares when they simplify the result.','For F(A,B,C)=Σm(1,3,5,7), all four selected rows have C=1 and include every A,B combination. One group of four eliminates A and B, so F=C.','What is the SOP grouping size allowed in a K-map?','A power of two',['Any odd integer','Only three cells','Only one cell'],'Each pairwise grouping removes one changing variable, so valid group sizes are 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on.'),
t('Adders, subtractors, comparators, multiplexers and decoders','A half adder uses XOR for sum and AND for carry. A full adder includes carry-in, so its carry is majority of the three inputs. A comparator checks equality or order, starting from the most significant differing bit. A multiplexer selects one data input and can implement a truth table using the select bits. A decoder activates one output per input code, while an encoder maps an active input to a code.','To implement F(A,B)=A XOR B in a 4:1 multiplexer, use A,B as selects and wire D₀,D₁,D₂,D₃ to 0,1,1,0. Select 10 then chooses D₂=1.','How many select bits does an 8-to-1 multiplexer need?','3',['2','4','8'],'Three bits represent eight possible channel numbers.'),
t('Latches, SR, JK, D and T flip-flops','A latch responds throughout its enable level, while an edge-triggered flip-flop samples at the clock edge. D transfers its data to Q. T toggles when T=1. JK holds for 00, resets for 01, sets for 10 and toggles for 11. An SR circuit has a forbidden simultaneous assertion in its basic implementation. Use excitation tables to find the inputs needed for a desired transition.','For a JK flip-flop initially at Q=0, take J=K=1 for three rising clock edges. Q toggles to 1, then 0, then 1.','What does a JK flip-flop do at its active edge when J=K=1?','Toggles',['Holds','Always sets','Always resets'],'Both asserted inputs select next state ¬Q.'),
t('Synchronous, ripple, mod-N, ring and Johnson counters','A ripple counter clocks each stage from the previous one, so transitions propagate through stages. A synchronous counter clocks all stages together and calculates their next states in logic. A mod-N counter repeats after N states and needs at least ⌈log₂N⌉ flip-flops. A ring counter circulates a one-hot pattern, while a Johnson counter feeds back the inverted final bit and can traverse 2m states with m stages.','A mod-10 counter requires four flip-flops because 2³<10≤2⁴. A synchronous implementation advances through 0000 to 1001 and declares the next state after 1001 as 0000.','How many states does an initialized five-stage Johnson counter cycle through?','10',['5','16','32'],'Inverted feedback fills the register with ones and then empties it, giving 2n states.'),
t('Shift registers, serial/parallel conversion and state machines','A shift register moves each stored bit one stage per clock and can convert serial input to parallel output or perform the reverse. A finite-state machine computes its next state from the present state and inputs. A Moore output depends on state, while a Mealy output depends on state and current input. Build a state table before assigning binary codes.','A Mealy detector for 01 stores whether the previous input was 0. On input 1 in the stored-zero state, it emits 1 and clears the state. For input 001, its output is 001.','Which FSM model can change output when the input changes before a state transition?','Mealy',['Moore only','Both models require a state change','Neither model has inputs'],'Mealy outputs are functions of both present state and current input.'),
t('Timing, logic families, fan-out and noise margin','Setup time is the required valid interval before a clock edge and hold time is the interval after it. For a same-clock register path without skew, the cycle must cover maximum clock-to-Q delay, combinational delay and setup. The minimum path must cover hold time. Increasing clock period can address setup failure, while hold violations typically require changing the short data path.','With clock-to-Q 1 ns, critical logic delay 5 ns and setup 1 ns, the minimum period is 7 ns and maximum frequency is about 143 MHz.','Which interval requires data to remain stable after the sampling edge?','Hold time',['Setup time','Clock period','Rise time'],'Hold specifies the minimum required stability immediately after the clock edge.'),
t('ROM, RAM, SRAM, DRAM and programmable logic','ROM holds fixed lookup data during normal operation. SRAM uses a bistable cell and maintains data while powered without refresh. DRAM stores charge in a capacitor and refreshes it periodically, enabling higher density. An address decoder selects the location and word width controls how many bits are accessed together.','A memory with 12 address lines and 8 bits per location has 2¹²=4096 words and stores 32,768 bits, or 4 KiB.','Which volatile memory typically requires periodic refresh?','DRAM',['SRAM','ROM','Combinational LUT output'],'Charge stored in DRAM cells leaks over time and must be restored.'),
t('ADC, DAC, quantization, flash, SAR and R–2R ladders','A sample-and-hold keeps input voltage steady during conversion. An ideal N-bit ADC divides its input span into 2ᴺ bins, so the nominal step is span/2ᴺ. Rounding has a quantization error up to about half a step away from overload. A flash ADC compares against all thresholds in parallel. A SAR ADC tries bits from most to least significant using a comparator and internal DAC. An R–2R DAC uses a repeated resistor ladder to produce binary weights.','An 8-bit ADC over a 0–5 V span has a nominal step of 5/256≈19.53 mV. Ideal rounding uncertainty is approximately ±9.77 mV within range.','How many comparators does a conventional 3-bit flash ADC need?','7',['3','8','4'],'A flash ADC uses one threshold between adjacent levels, giving 2³−1 comparators.')
],[['NAND','Universal gate'],['Johnson counter','Inverted shift-register feedback'],['Mealy model','Output depends on input and state'],['SAR ADC','Trials proceed from most to least significant bit']]),

s('signals','Signals and Systems',[
t('Signal types, periodicity, symmetry, energy and power','A continuous-time signal x(t) is defined at every real time t, while a discrete-time signal x[n] is indexed by integers. Discrete time and discrete amplitude are separate properties. Periodicity requires x(t+T)=x(t) for all t, or x[n+N]=x[n] for all n with N a positive integer. The fundamental period is the smallest positive one. A discrete sinusoid at angular frequency Ω is periodic when Ω/(2π) is rational. The even and odd parts are (x(t)+x(−t))/2 and (x(t)−x(−t))/2. Integrate |x(t)|² over all time for energy, or sum |x[n]|² for a sequence. Average power is the limit of that same quantity over a symmetric window divided by its duration or sample count. A nonzero finite-energy signal has zero average power, and a nonzero periodic signal with finite energy per period has positive average power and infinite total energy.','For x[n]=2cos(πn/3), solve (π/3)N=2πk. The smallest positive N is 6. Since cosine is even, x[−n]=x[n]. The six values are 2, 1, −1, −2, −1, 1, so average power is (4+1+1+4+1+1)/6=2. The same nonzero energy repeats every period, so total energy diverges.','A unit-amplitude sine exists for all time. What type of signal is it?','A power signal',['A finite-energy signal','A zero-power signal','An aperiodic signal'],'It has average power 1/2 and infinite total energy.'),
t('Impulse, step, ramp, exponential and sinusoid','The unit step u(t) is zero for t<0 and one for t>0. A shifted step locates an onset, and the difference of two steps creates a finite pulse. The ramp r(t)=tu(t) starts at zero and rises with unit slope. The continuous impulse δ(t) is a distribution with unit area, and integrating x(t)δ(t−a) gives x(a) when x is continuous at a. The discrete impulse δ[n] equals one at n=0 and zero elsewhere. Differentiating the continuous step gives δ(t), while differencing u[n]−u[n−1] gives δ[n]. A real exponential Aeᵃᵗ grows or decays according to the sign of a. A sinusoid A cos(ωt+φ) has amplitude |A|, period 2π/|ω| for nonzero ω, and phase φ.','Let x(t)=3[u(t−1)−u(t−4)]. For 1<t<4, only the first step is on, so x=3. Elsewhere the steps are equal, so x=0. Integrating gives 3[r(t−1)−r(t−4)], which rises from 0 to 9 and stays at 9. Differentiating x gives 3δ(t−1)−3δ(t−4), one impulse per jump.','What does convolution with δ(t−3) do to x(t)?','Delays x(t) by 3',['Advances x(t) by 3','Scales its amplitude by 3','Differentiates it'],'A shifted impulse implements the same time shift under convolution.'),
t('Time shifting, scaling, reversal and signal operations','Amplitude scaling changes sample values and time scaling changes the argument. The expression x(t−b) delays by b when b>0, x(at) compresses when |a|>1 and expands when 0<|a|<1, and a<0 reverses the order of features. For x(at+b), map each original feature at τ to t=(τ−b)/a and reorder the endpoints if a<0. To add or multiply signals, first align them on the same time axis, then combine values at each time. In discrete time, evaluated indices must be integers. In particular x[Mn] keeps every Mth sample and introduces no intermediate values.','Suppose x(t)=1 on 1≤t≤5 and zero elsewhere. For y(t)=2x(3−2t), require 1≤3−2t≤5. Solving gives −1≤t≤1. The original right edge maps to −1 and the left edge maps to 1, so the pulse is reversed, half as wide, and twice as high.','For x(2t), what happens to the duration of a pulse?','It halves',['It doubles','It stays fixed','It becomes zero'],'The input argument reaches each feature in half the original time.'),
t('Linearity, time invariance, causality, memory and BIBO','A system T is linear if T{ax₁+bx₂}=aT{x₁}+bT{x₂} for arbitrary inputs and constants. It is time invariant if shifting an input by τ shifts its output by the same τ. A causal system at t₀ can use inputs up to t₀. A memoryless system uses only x(t₀). BIBO stability requires a bounded output for every bounded input. These tests are independent. For example, time invariance must be checked by applying the shift both before and after the system and comparing the results.','For y(t)=t x(t), multiplication by t distributes over any weighted sum, so the system is linear. It uses only x(t), so it is causal and memoryless. A delayed input gives t x(t−τ), whereas delaying the output gives (t−τ)x(t−τ), so it is time varying. The bounded input x=1 gives y=t, so it fails BIBO stability.','Is y(t)=x(t+1) causal?','No, it requires a future input value',['Yes because it is linear','Yes because it is time-invariant','Yes because its gain is one'],'At time t it depends on x one unit later than t.'),
t('LTI systems, impulse/step responses and convolution','An LTI system turns each shifted input impulse into the same shifted copy of its impulse response h. Summing those responses gives y[n]=Σₖ x[k]h[n−k], or y(t)=∫ x(τ)h(t−τ)dτ in continuous time. For a graphical convolution, reverse and shift h, multiply by x, then sum or integrate over the overlap. The step response is h∗u, so differentiation in continuous time or first differencing in discrete time recovers h. Causality requires h to vanish at negative times. BIBO stability requires absolute integrability for an ordinary continuous-time h, or absolute summability in discrete time.','Let x[n]={1,2} and h[n]={1,−1}, both starting at n=0. At n=0, y=1·1=1. At n=1, y=1·(−1)+2·1=1. At n=2, y=2·(−1)=−2. The output is {1,1,−2}. Since h[n]=δ[n]−δ[n−1], the same result follows from y[n]=x[n]−x[n−1].','Which operation gives the output of an LTI system?','Convolution of input and impulse response',['Pointwise multiplication of both time signals','Division by the impulse response','Differentiation of input in every case'],'An LTI output is a weighted sum of shifted impulse responses.'),
t('Fourier series, Fourier transform and frequency spectra','For a signal periodic with period T, the complex Fourier series uses harmonics eʲ²πᵏᵗ/ᵀ. Obtain Cₖ by multiplying x(t) by the conjugate harmonic, integrating over one period, and dividing by T. For an aperiodic signal, the transform X(f)=∫x(t)e⁻ʲ²πᶠᵗdt describes a continuous spectrum where the integral exists. A periodic waveform has a line spectrum at the harmonics. Delaying by τ multiplies the spectrum by e⁻ʲ²πᶠτ, changing phase but preserving magnitude. Convolution in time becomes multiplication in frequency. For a system with phase θ(ω), phase delay is −θ/ω and group delay is −dθ/dω.','For x(t)=4cos(2π·100t), Euler’s formula writes the cosine as two complex exponentials with coefficient 2 at ±100 Hz. Delay it by 1 ms. The positive-frequency coefficient becomes 2e⁻ʲ⁰·²π and the negative-frequency coefficient becomes 2eʲ⁰·²π. Both magnitudes stay 2, and the phase slope corresponds to a 1 ms group delay.','How does a pure time delay change Fourier magnitude?','It leaves it unchanged',['It halves it','It shifts it to positive frequency only','It always doubles it'],'The frequency multiplier for a delay has unit magnitude.'),
t('Laplace transform, ROC, poles, zeros and inverse transform','The bilateral Laplace transform X(s)=∫x(t)e⁻ˢᵗdt introduces the complex variable s=σ+jω. Its region of convergence (ROC) records the values of s for which the integral converges. For a rational transform, poles and zeros are roots of the denominator and numerator after cancellation. Right-sided exponentials converge to the right of their poles, left-sided exponentials converge to the left, and two-sided signals can have a strip as their ROC. The ROC excludes poles. To invert, use partial fractions and choose the time support of each term from the ROC. Evaluate at s=jω for the Fourier transform when the imaginary axis belongs to the ROC.','For x(t)=e⁻²ᵗu(t), integrate e⁻⁽ˢ⁺²⁾ᵗ from 0 to ∞ to obtain 1/(s+2), valid when Re(s)>−2. If 1/(s+2) is given with Re(s)<−2 instead, integration over negative time gives the inverse −e⁻²ᵗu(−t). The pole is the same, while the ROC determines which waveform it represents.','Can the same bilateral Laplace expression represent two signals with different ROCs?','Yes',['No','Only if it contains no poles','Only for sampled signals'],'A distinct ROC can correspond to a distinct time support, such as left-sided versus right-sided.'),
t('Z-transform, ROC, inverse and difference equations','The bilateral Z-transform is X(z)=Σₙx[n]z⁻ⁿ. Its ROC is a ring in the complex plane, possibly extending to zero or infinity, and it excludes poles. A right-sided rational sequence has an exterior ROC, while a left-sided one has an interior ROC. For an LTI system, a causal rational impulse response uses the exterior ROC, and stability requires the unit circle to lie in the ROC. Evaluating on that circle gives the DTFT when it converges. Under initial rest, delay by one sample multiplies the transform by z⁻¹. Use this rule to solve a difference equation algebraically, then apply the inverse transform with the corresponding ROC.','For y[n]−0.5y[n−1]=x[n] under initial rest, (1−0.5z⁻¹)Y=X, so H(z)=1/(1−0.5z⁻¹). Expanding as a geometric series gives h[n]=(0.5)ⁿu[n]. The causal ROC |z|>0.5 includes the unit circle, so the system is stable.','A causal rational discrete LTI system has a pole at z=1.2. Is it BIBO stable?','No',['Yes','Only if the pole is real','Only if the input is periodic'],'The causal ROC excludes the unit circle in this case.'),
t('Sampling, Nyquist rate, aliasing and reconstruction','Sampling x(t) every Tₛ seconds produces x[n]=x(nTₛ), with sample rate fₛ=1/Tₛ. Its spectrum contains copies of the original spectrum spaced fₛ apart. If the signal is bandlimited to B Hz, choosing fₛ>2B keeps these copies separated. The Nyquist rate is 2B, whereas the Nyquist frequency for a given sampler is fₛ/2. When copies overlap, different analog frequencies can yield the same samples. Fold a real sinusoid to the interval from 0 to fₛ/2 by subtracting an integer multiple of fₛ and taking the absolute value. Ideal sinc interpolation reconstructs a bandlimited signal from sufficient samples. Practical systems use an analog anti-alias filter before sampling.','Sample x(t)=cos(2π·900t) at 1000 Hz. Then x[n]=cos(2π·0.9n)=cos(2π·(−0.1)n)=cos(2π·0.1n). The samples therefore look exactly like a 100 Hz cosine. To keep a 900 Hz baseband component unaliased, use a rate above 1800 samples/s and preserve room for the filter transition.','What is the Nyquist rate for a baseband signal bandlimited to 3 kHz?','6 ksample/s',['3 ksample/s','1.5 ksample/s','9 ksample/s'],'The theoretical threshold is twice the highest signal frequency.'),
t('Correlation, energy spectra and power spectra','Autocorrelation measures similarity to a shifted conjugate copy of the same signal. Cross-correlation compares two signals and can locate their relative delay. For a finite-energy signal, Rₓₓ(τ)=∫x(t)x*(t−τ)dt and Rₓₓ(0) is total energy. Fourier transforming that autocorrelation gives the energy spectral density |X(f)|². For a wide-sense stationary process, take the expectation of the lagged product instead. Its autocorrelation depends only on lag, its zero-lag value gives average power, and its Fourier transform is the power spectral density. Filtering a stationary process through an LTI system multiplies its PSD by |H(f)|².','Let x(t)=2 for 0≤t≤3 and zero elsewhere. Its shifted copy overlaps over max(3−|τ|,0) seconds, with product 4 in the overlap. Thus Rₓₓ(τ)=4max(3−|τ|,0). At τ=0 this gives 12, equal to the energy 4·3.','What does zero-lag autocorrelation equal for a deterministic energy signal?','Its total energy',['Its bandwidth','Its peak sample only','Its phase delay'],'At zero lag the signal is integrated against its own conjugate.')
],[['ROC','Determines convergence and helps identify time support'],['Autocorrelation','Compares a waveform with delayed versions of itself'],['Time invariance','Output shifts by the same delay as the input'],['BIBO stability','All bounded inputs produce bounded outputs']]),

s('dsp','Digital Signal Processing',[
t('DTFT and discrete-time frequency response','The DTFT is X(eʲω)=Σₙx[n]e⁻ʲωⁿ with ω in radians per sample. Adding 2π to ω changes no integer-index exponential, so one period contains all distinct frequencies. Complex exponentials are eigenfunctions of LTI systems: H(eʲω) multiplies each input component at that frequency. Its magnitude sets amplitude gain and its phase sets phase shift.','For h[n]=δ[n]+δ[n−1], H=1+e⁻ʲω. At ω=π, H=1−1=0, so the alternating sequence (−1)ⁿ is rejected.','What is the period of the DTFT in angular frequency?','2π',['π','1','It is aperiodic'],'Multiplying ω by an offset of 2π leaves exp(−jωn) unchanged for integer n.'),
t('DFT, inverse DFT, bins and frequency spacing','The N-point DFT samples the DTFT of an N-sample record at ωₖ=2πk/N. Compute X[k]=Σₙx[n]e⁻ʲ²πᵏⁿ/ᴺ and invert with the opposite sign and a factor 1/N. Bin spacing in Hz is fₛ/N. Bins above N/2 represent negative frequencies under the usual signed convention. Padding adds a denser sampling of the same windowed spectrum and leaves the information in the original record unchanged.','For N=8 and fₛ=800 Hz, spacing is 100 Hz. A real cosine at 200 Hz lands in bins k=2 and k=6, representing +200 and −200 Hz. With unit amplitude and zero initial phase, both DFT values are N/2=4.','At fs=1 kHz and N=200, what is the bin spacing?','5 Hz',['0.2 Hz','50 Hz','200 Hz'],'Divide sample rate by DFT length.'),
t('Linear versus circular convolution and zero-padding','Linear convolution uses zero extension outside a sequence. Circular convolution uses indices modulo N. Multiplying two N-point DFTs and inverting gives their N-point circular convolution. To compute a linear convolution of lengths L and M this way, pad both to at least L+M−1 before taking the DFT. Shorter FFT lengths can fold a tail back into the beginning.','For x={1,2} and h={1,1}, linear convolution is {1,3,2}. A two-point circular convolution folds the last 2 onto the first index and yields {3,3}. A three-point DFT of both zero-padded sequences preserves {1,3,2}.','What is the smallest padding length for convolution of sequences of lengths 4 and 6?','9',['6','8','10'],'The linear-convolution output length is 4+6−1.'),
t('Radix-2 FFT and computational cost','The radix-2 FFT computes the DFT by splitting a power-of-two record into its even and odd samples. At each stage combine the two half-length transforms with twiddle factors e⁻ʲ²πᵏ/ᴺ. One butterfly produces the sum and difference for output bins k and k+N/2. Repeating the split gives log₂N stages with N/2 butterflies each, reducing arithmetic growth from O(N²) to O(N log N).','For x={1,2,3,4}, the even samples {1,3} give {4,−2}, and the odd samples {2,4} give {6,−2}. The first twiddle is 1, so bins 0 and 2 are 10 and −2. The second twiddle is −j, so bins 1 and 3 are −2+2j and −2−2j.','Does an FFT change the DFT values?','No, it is a faster algorithm for the same transform',['Yes, it removes every odd bin','Yes, it computes a different transform','Yes, it always doubles spectral resolution'],'The computational structure changes, while the mathematical output is the DFT.'),
t('FIR and IIR filters, stability, phase and direct forms','An FIR filter forms a finite weighted sum of present and past inputs. Its finite impulse response guarantees BIBO stability for finite coefficients. A real symmetric or antisymmetric FIR can have linear phase with constant group delay. An IIR filter feeds back output samples and generally has an infinite impulse response. A causal rational IIR is stable when its poles lie inside the unit circle after cancellations. Direct form I uses separate input and output delay lines, while direct form II shares delays.','The three-tap filter y[n]=(x[n]+2x[n−1]+x[n−2])/4 is FIR. Its frequency response is e⁻ʲω(1+cosω)/2. The phase term gives a one-sample delay, the gain at DC is 1, and the gain at ω=π is zero.','Where must the poles of a stable causal rational IIR lie?','Strictly inside the unit circle',['Strictly outside the unit circle','Exactly on the unit circle','In the left half-plane only'],'For the causal ROC to include the unit circle, all finite poles must have magnitude below one.'),
t('Windowing, leakage and basic filter design','Multiplying a signal by a finite observation window convolves its spectrum with the window spectrum. A narrow main lobe helps resolve nearby tones, while low sidelobes suppress leakage from stronger tones. In FIR window design, truncate and window the ideal impulse response. For a low-pass cutoff ω꜀, the centered ideal samples are sin(ω꜀n)/(πn) with center value ω꜀/π. Shifting the windowed taps makes the filter causal.','For a three-tap rectangular-window low-pass with ω꜀=π/2, keep n=−1,0,1. The ideal samples are {1/π,1/2,1/π}. Move them to indices 0 through 2 for a causal FIR with one sample of delay.','Which is a common consequence of a tapered window?','Lower sidelobes and wider main lobe',['Every tone becomes a single bin','Higher sidelobes and always narrower main lobe','The sample rate doubles'],'Tapering makes record ends smoother, reducing sidelobes while sacrificing some resolution.'),
t('Interpolation and decimation','Upsampling by L inserts L−1 zeros between samples, then a low-pass filter suppresses spectral images and reconstructs intermediate samples. Downsampling by M keeps every Mth sample. An anti-alias low-pass filter normally comes before downsampling. Multirate implementations can combine these operations efficiently.','Decimating 48 ksample/s by 3 gives 16 ksample/s. Filter the input so frequencies at or above the new 8 kHz Nyquist limit do not alias.','Where should the anti-alias filter be placed in a decimator?','Before downsampling',['After downsampling only','After the DFT only','It is unnecessary for every input'],'Once spectral components alias during downsampling, a later filter cannot undo overlap.')
],[['FFT','Efficient DFT algorithm'],['Zero-padding for convolution','Prevents cyclic wraparound at adequate length'],['FIR','Finite impulse response'],['Decimation','Reduces the sample rate']]),

s('control','Control Systems',[
t('Open and closed loops, modelling and transfer functions','A plant converts a control input into a physical output. Open-loop control drives the plant from the command alone. Feedback subtracts a measured output from the reference and acts on the error. Model the plant with differential equations, then take Laplace transforms under zero initial conditions to get its transfer function. For negative feedback with plant G and sensor H, Y/R=G/(1+GH). The denominator governs closed-loop poles.','For G=8/(s+2) and unity feedback, Y/R=8/(s+10). A unit step settles at 0.8 with time constant 0.1 s.','Which loop measures its output to correct the input?','Closed loop',['Open loop','Feedforward only','Purely disconnected loop'],'Feedback uses measured output to form an error signal.'),
t('Block diagrams, signal-flow graphs and Mason’s formula','Multiply blocks in cascade and add signed gains for parallel paths. A feedback loop reduces to G/(1+GH) for negative feedback. A signal-flow graph expresses each variable as a weighted sum of incoming branches. Mason’s rule adds each forward-path gain times its cofactor, then divides by Δ=1−sum of loop gains+sum of products of disjoint loop pairs−⋯. A cofactor excludes loops touching that path.','A single forward path of gain 4 and a touching loop of gain −0.5 give Δ=1−(−0.5)=1.5 and path cofactor 1, so the overall gain is 4/1.5=8/3.','Which loop pairs contribute to the second-order product terms of Mason’s Δ?','Pairs of non-touching loops',['Pairs sharing a node','Any two paths','Only loops on the forward path'],'Only loops with no common node count as non-touching.'),
t('Poles, zeros, first- and second-order systems and test inputs','A transfer function’s zeros shape how modes appear at the output, while its poles set the natural modes. A stable real pole at −a gives a term e⁻ᵃᵗ. Complex conjugate poles −ζωₙ±jωₙ√(1−ζ²) give damped oscillation for 0<ζ<1. Standard test inputs include impulse, step and ramp with transforms 1, 1/s and 1/s². Multiply the input transform by G(s) and invert to get the response under initial rest.','For G(s)=4/(s+4) and unit step, Y=4/[s(s+4)]=1/s−1/(s+4). Hence y(t)=1−e⁻⁴ᵗ for t≥0.','What is the pole of a first-order system with time constant 4 s?','−0.25 s⁻¹',['+0.25 s⁻¹','−4 s⁻¹','+4 s⁻¹'],'The pole is −1/τ.'),
t('Time response metrics and damping','A first-order step response reaches 63.2% of its final change in one time constant. For a standard underdamped second-order response, ωᴅ=ωₙ√(1−ζ²), peak time is π/ωᴅ and percent overshoot is 100e⁻πζ/√(1−ζ²). The 2% settling estimate is 4/(ζωₙ). Rise time depends on the threshold convention and should be read using the definition in the problem.','With ζ=0.5 and ωₙ=10 rad/s, ωᴅ=8.66 rad/s, peak time≈0.363 s, overshoot≈16.3%, and 2% settling time≈0.8 s.','At fixed ωₙ, what usually happens to percent overshoot when ζ rises within 0<ζ<1?','It decreases',['It increases','It stays constant','It equals the rise time'],'Greater damping reduces the peak above the final value.'),
t('Steady-state error, system type and error constants','For stable unity feedback, E(s)=R(s)/(1+G(s)). The final-value theorem gives eₛₛ=limₛ→₀sE(s) when its pole condition is satisfied. The system type is the count of uncancelled poles at the origin in the loop transfer. For unit step, ramp and parabolic inputs, use Kₚ=lim G, Kᵥ=lim sG and Kₐ=lim s²G, giving errors 1/(1+Kₚ), 1/Kᵥ and 1/Kₐ.','For G(s)=10/[s(s+2)], the unity-feedback characteristic polynomial s²+2s+10 is stable. Kᵥ=10/2=5, so unit ramp error is 0.2. The loop has one integrator, giving zero step error.','Which error constant controls unit-ramp error?','Kv',['Kp','Ka','Gain margin'],'A unit ramp has Laplace transform 1/s², so the final-value limit involves sG(s).'),
t('Routh–Hurwitz, relative stability and root locus','The Routh array tests the closed-loop characteristic polynomial. Sign changes in the first column count right-half-plane roots, with special handling for a zero pivot or a row of zeros. Root locus traces closed-loop poles as a gain varies, starting at open-loop poles and ending at zeros or infinity. On the real axis, points with an odd number of real open-loop poles and zeros to their right belong to the positive-gain locus.','For s³+3s²+2s+K, the Routh first column is 1, 3, (6−K)/3, K. Strict positivity gives stability for 0<K<6.','Where do conventional positive-gain root-locus branches start as gain tends to zero?','Open-loop poles',['Open-loop zeros only','Gain-crossover frequencies','The imaginary axis in every case'],'At zero gain, the closed-loop characteristic roots reduce to the open-loop pole locations.'),
t('Bode plots, margins and Nyquist stability','A Bode plot shows loop gain in decibels and phase versus frequency. At unity gain, phase margin is 180° plus the phase. At −180°, gain margin is the reciprocal of the linear gain there. Nyquist extends the stability test to loops with unstable open-loop poles by counting signed encirclements of −1. Use the complete contour and a consistent encirclement sign convention.','For a loop whose only gain crossover has phase −135°, the phase margin is 45°. If its −180° crossover has magnitude 0.25, the gain margin is 4, or 20log₁₀4≈12.0 dB.','At which frequency do you read phase margin?','Gain crossover',['Phase crossover','Natural frequency in all cases','DC only'],'Phase margin is computed where the loop magnitude equals one.'),
t('P, PI, PD, PID and lead–lag compensation','A P term multiplies present error, I accumulates error, and D acts on its rate of change. An integrator can remove step tracking error in a stable loop, while derivative action can add damping but amplifies high-frequency noise unless filtered. Lead compensation adds positive phase over a frequency band. Lag compensation can increase low-frequency gain relative to crossover gain. After choosing compensator gains, check closed-loop stability and response.','For plant G=1/(s+1), choose PI C=(2s+2)/s. The loop reduces to 2/s and the unity-feedback command response is 2/(s+2). Its DC gain is one and its time constant is 0.5 s.','Which compensator is commonly used to add positive phase near crossover?','Lead',['Lag only','Pure integrator','Dead zone'],'A lead network places its zero at a lower break frequency than its pole.'),
t('State equations, controllability and observability','State equations ẋ=Ax+Bu and y=Cx+Du represent internal dynamics as first-order equations. The homogeneous solution is eᴬᵗx(0). The forced solution integrates eᴬ⁽ᵗ⁻τ⁾Bu(τ) over the input history. Full rank of [B AB …] means the input can drive every state direction. Full column rank of the stacked matrices C, CA and higher powers means the output can reveal every initial state.','For A=diag(−1,−2) and x(0)=(3,4) with zero input, eᴬᵗ=diag(e⁻ᵗ,e⁻²ᵗ). Thus x(t)=(3e⁻ᵗ,4e⁻²ᵗ), showing the two independent decay rates.','Which matrix starts the controllability test?','B',['C','D','The identity in all systems'],'B describes how inputs directly act on states, and powers of A reveal indirect effects.')
],[['Phase margin','Read at gain crossover'],['Routh sign changes','Count right-half-plane roots'],['Observability','Infer internal state from measured output'],['PI controller','Proportional action plus integration']]),

s('comm','Communication Systems',[
t('Baseband, passband, carriers, spectrum and rates','A baseband message occupies frequencies near zero. Modulation puts its spectrum around a carrier to fit a passband channel. Symbol rate counts transmitted symbols each second. For uncoded M-ary signalling with equally sized binary labels, each symbol carries log₂M bits. Bandwidth depends on symbol rate, pulse shape and modulation.','An uncoded 16-QAM link at 1 Msymbol/s carries 1·log₂16=4 Mbit/s. With raised-cosine roll-off 0.25 its nominal RF bandwidth is 1.25 MHz.','How many bits per symbol does 16-QAM carry?','4',['2','8','16'],'There are 16 = 2⁴ distinct symbols.'),
t('Noise, thermal noise, SNR, noise figure and Shannon capacity','Thermal noise has available power kTB over bandwidth B at temperature T. Noise figure is the input SNR divided by output SNR. For an AWGN channel, capacity is B log₂(1+S/N) with linear SNR. Entropy H=−Σp log₂p gives average information per symbol, lossless source coding approaches that average length, and mutual information measures what observations reveal about inputs.','For B=1 MHz and SNR=15 in linear units, capacity is 10⁶log₂16=4 Mbit/s. A rate above this limit cannot achieve arbitrarily low error in that channel model.','Which SNR value goes into Shannon’s logarithm for a 20 dB SNR?','100',['20','2','200'],'Convert with 10 raised to SNRdB/10.'),
t('AM, DSB-SC, SSB, VSB, sidebands and power','Conventional single-tone AM sends a carrier and sidebands at f꜀±fₘ. Its envelope follows the message if modulation depth m≤1. DSB-SC suppresses the carrier and typically uses coherent recovery. SSB transmits one sideband and requires half the DSB bandwidth. For conventional AM, total power is P꜀(1+m²/2) when the message is a sinusoid.','With 100 W carrier power and m=0.8, each sideband has 100·0.8²/4=16 W. Total transmitted power is 132 W.','How much bandwidth does ideal SSB need for a 4 kHz low-pass message?','4 kHz',['2 kHz','8 kHz','12 kHz'],'Only one translated sideband remains.'),
t('FM, PM, deviation, Carson’s rule and demodulation','Angle modulation varies carrier phase. In PM, phase deviation is proportional to the message. In FM, frequency deviation is proportional to the message, so phase depends on its integral. For a tone, the FM index is Δf/fₘ. FM has theoretically many sidebands. Carson’s rule estimates useful bandwidth by 2(Δf+Bₘ), and a discriminator recovers deviations as voltage.','For peak deviation 75 kHz and a highest message frequency of 15 kHz, Carson’s estimate is 2(75+15)=180 kHz. For a 15 kHz tone the modulation index is 5.','In single-tone FM, Δf=30 kHz and fm=10 kHz. What is β?','3',['0.33','20','40'],'FM modulation index is frequency deviation divided by tone frequency.'),
t('Superheterodyne receiver, mixer, LO, IF and image','A mixer generates sum and difference frequencies. A superheterodyne receiver chooses an IF where filtering and amplification are easier to implement. A second RF frequency on the other side of the LO can generate the same IF. An RF preselector helps reject this image before mixing.','To receive 100 MHz with a 10.7 MHz IF and high-side LO, choose fᴸᴼ=110.7 MHz. The image is 121.4 MHz because 121.4−110.7=10.7 MHz.','With desired RF 100 MHz and high-side LO 110 MHz, where is the image?','120 MHz',['90 MHz','100 MHz','110 MHz'],'Both 100 MHz and 120 MHz are 10 MHz from the LO.'),
t('PAM, PWM, PPM, PCM, DPCM, delta modulation and companding','PCM samples the message, quantizes each level and encodes the quantizer index into bits. For L uniform levels and span V, the step is V/L. N bits per sample make L=2ᴺ levels and gross bit rate Nfₛ for one channel. DPCM encodes the difference between a sample and its prediction, potentially reducing the number of bits for a given fidelity when samples are correlated.','At 8 ksample/s with 8 bits per sample, PCM uses 256 levels and carries 64 kbit/s before framing. On a 2 V span the step is 2/256≈7.81 mV.','What quantity does DPCM primarily quantize?','Prediction error',['Absolute carrier phase','Only the largest input sample','The pulse repetition interval'],'Encoding the difference from a prediction can reduce redundancy.'),
t('Line coding, NRZ, RZ, Manchester and ISI','Intersymbol interference occurs when pulse tails contribute at other symbols’ sampling instants. A raised-cosine overall impulse response has zeros at nonzero integer symbol intervals and thus no ISI at the correct sample times. Its roll-off controls the excess bandwidth above the ideal Nyquist minimum. Clock recovery matters because a timing offset moves the sampler away from those zeros.','At 100 ksymbol/s with roll-off 0.4, the one-sided baseband bandwidth is (1+0.4)·100/2=70 kHz. Ideal symbol-spaced samples see zero contribution from neighboring symbols.','What is a defining feature of Manchester coding?','A transition at the midpoint of every bit',['No transitions for repeated bits','Two bits per amplitude level only','A forced zero level at the end of all bits'],'That mid-bit transition provides a timing cue.'),
t('ASK, FSK, BPSK, QPSK, QAM, I/Q and BER','ASK changes amplitude, FSK changes frequency, and PSK changes phase. QAM uses independently weighted in-phase and quadrature carriers, with one constellation point per symbol. Signal scaling, noise and constellation spacing determine decision reliability. Gray labeling makes nearest neighbors differ by one bit. For coherent BPSK over AWGN, BER=Q(√(2Eᵦ/N₀)).','At Eᵦ/N₀=4 in linear units, coherent BPSK has BER=Q(√8)≈0.00234. Doubling bit energy at fixed noise density raises Eᵦ/N₀ by about 3 dB.','Which modulation changes the transmitted frequency according to the symbol?','FSK',['ASK','BPSK','QAM'],'Frequency shift keying selects among discrete frequency choices.'),
t('Coherent detection, matched filtering and Nyquist zero ISI','A matched filter correlates the received waveform with the expected pulse and maximizes sampled SNR in white noise. Maximum-likelihood detection picks the hypothesis with highest likelihood. MAP detection weights likelihood by prior probability, agreeing with ML for equal priors. The Nyquist zero-ISI test applies to the overall transmit-to-sample response.','For equally likely BPSK symbols ±1 with AWGN, ML picks the nearest point. A matched-filter sample of −0.3 is 0.7 from −1 and 1.3 from +1, so decide −1.','What does a matched filter maximize in AWGN?','Output SNR at the decision sampling instant',['Carrier frequency','Occupied bandwidth','The number of symbol errors'],'A pulse-matched impulse response exploits the known signal shape.'),
t('FDM, TDM, CDMA, OFDM, FFT and cyclic prefix','FDM allocates frequency bands, TDM time slots, and CDMA spreading codes. OFDM uses orthogonal subcarriers spaced by 1/Tu and typically uses an IFFT at transmit and FFT at receive. The cyclic prefix copies the end of the useful symbol to its beginning. With adequate prefix duration and timing, the channel convolution can be handled as circular over the useful block.','With Tu=64 µs, subcarriers are 15.625 kHz apart. A 16 µs prefix consumes 20% of total symbol time.','What part of the OFDM symbol determines subcarrier spacing?','Useful symbol duration',['Cyclic prefix duration alone','Guard interval plus useful duration in every case','Number of pilot symbols alone'],'Orthogonality is measured over the useful FFT interval.'),
t('Parity, CRC, Hamming distance and error correction','Minimum Hamming distance dₘᵢₙ is the least number of bit differences between valid codewords. It permits detection of up to dₘᵢₙ−1 errors or correction of ⌊(dₘᵢₙ−1)/2⌋ errors under their respective decoding goals. Hamming codes use parity-check syndromes to locate single-bit errors. A CRC appends a remainder from binary polynomial division and verifies it at reception.','For the Hamming (7,4) code, dₘᵢₙ=3 and one bit can be corrected. With standard parity positions 1, 2 and 4, a syndrome 101₂ indicates bit 5, so flipping bit 5 restores the valid codeword under the single-error assumption.','How many errors can a minimum-distance-5 code always correct?','2',['1','4','5'],'Take the floor of (5−1)/2.'),
t('Wireless channels, fading, Doppler, diversity and MIMO','Multipath gives delayed replicas, and delay spread can cause frequency selectivity. Motion changes the observed frequency through Doppler. Rayleigh fading models a diffuse channel without a dominant component, and Rician adds a dominant component. Diversity combines independent observations for reliability. MIMO can steer energy through beamforming or send parallel streams through spatial multiplexing when the channel supports them.','In an indoor link, two paths can cancel at one location. Moving the antenna or combining a second branch can reduce the chance that both observations fade together.','Which MIMO technique sends different data streams in parallel?','Spatial multiplexing',['Receive diversity alone','Cyclic prefixing','Carrier frequency recovery'],'Spatial multiplexing uses separate spatial modes for throughput.')
],[['SSB','Transmits one sideband'],['Matched filter','Maximizes SNR at a decision instant in AWGN'],['Cyclic prefix','Copies the end of an OFDM symbol to its front'],['Rician fading','Diffuse components plus a dominant component']]),

s('em','Electromagnetics, Transmission Lines, and RF',[
t('Electric and magnetic fields, Coulomb, Gauss, Faraday and Maxwell','Gauss’s electric law links electric flux to enclosed charge. Gauss’s magnetic law gives zero net magnetic flux through a closed surface. Faraday’s law relates induced electric circulation to changing magnetic flux. Ampère–Maxwell relates magnetic circulation to conduction and displacement current. Use integral form with symmetry and differential form for local field relations. Combining the curl laws in a source-free uniform medium gives wave speed 1/√(με).','An ideal parallel-plate capacitor has a changing electric flux. If C=10 pF and dV/dt=10⁸ V/s, displacement current between the plates is C dV/dt=1 mA, equal to the lead current.','Which law relates induced emf to the time change of magnetic flux?','Faraday’s law',['Coulomb’s law','Gauss’s electric law','Ohm’s law'],'For a fixed loop the induced emf is the negative time derivative of flux linkage.'),
t('Boundary conditions, plane waves, polarization and power flow','Tangential E is continuous across an ordinary interface. Tangential H can jump by surface current, and normal D can jump by surface charge. In a uniform lossless plane wave, E, H and propagation direction are mutually perpendicular, with |E|/|H|=√(μ/ε). Polarization tracks the electric-field tip. The Poynting vector E×H gives power flux, and average power uses RMS fields or half the peak phasor product.','A free-space plane wave has electric field 37.7 V/m RMS. With impedance about 377 Ω, H is 0.1 A/m RMS and average power density is 3.77 W/m² along propagation.','Which vector gives instantaneous electromagnetic power-flow density?','E × H',['E + H','E · H','H − E'],'The cross product is the Poynting vector.'),
t('Propagation in dielectrics and conductors, skin depth and interfaces','In a lossless dielectric, phase velocity is 1/√(με). Phase velocity is ω/β and group velocity is dω/dβ, so dispersion can make them differ. A conductor attenuates a field with depth. For a good conductor, skin depth δ≈√(2/(ωμσ)). At normal incidence, the electric-field reflection coefficient is (η₂−η₁)/(η₂+η₁). Oblique refraction follows phase matching along the interface.','For normal incidence from free space into a lossless nonmagnetic dielectric with εᵣ=4, η₂=377/2 Ω. The field reflection coefficient is −1/3 and the reflected power fraction is 1/9.','How far must a field travel into a conductor to fall to about 37% of its surface amplitude?','One skin depth',['One wavelength in all cases','Two skin depths','One quarter wavelength in all cases'],'Amplitude decays exponentially and e⁻¹ is approximately 0.368.'),
t('Line parameters, characteristic impedance and input impedance','Per-unit-length R, L, G and C govern the line voltage and current. The propagation constant is √[(R+jωL)(G+jωC)] and the characteristic impedance is the square root of their ratio. On a lossless line, Z₀=√(L/C). A mismatched termination creates a reflected wave, so input impedance depends on line length. A lossless line’s input is Z₀(Zᴸ+jZ₀tanβl)/(Z₀+jZᴸtanβl).','A lossless 50 Ω half-wave line terminated in 100 Ω has βl=π and tanβl=0. Substitution gives Zᵢₙ=100 Ω, the same as the load.','What is the input impedance of a lossless 50 Ω line terminated in 50 Ω?','50 Ω for any length',['0 Ω','Always twice the load','It alternates between open and short'],'A matched termination produces no reflection.'),
t('Reflection, VSWR, return loss and matching','Load reflection coefficient Γ=(Zᴸ−Z₀)/(Zᴸ+Z₀). On a lossless line, standing waves give VSWR=(1+|Γ|)/(1−|Γ|). Return loss is −20log₁₀|Γ|, while reflected power fraction is |Γ|². Matching makes Γ zero at the target frequency, improving power transfer.','For Zᴸ=100 Ω on a 50 Ω line, Γ=1/3, VSWR=2 and return loss≈9.54 dB. The reflected power is 1/9 of the incident power.','If |Γ| is 0.5, how much incident power is reflected?','25%',['50%','75%','100%'],'Reflection coefficient is a voltage-wave ratio, so square its magnitude for power.'),
t('Open and short lines, quarter-wave transformation, Smith chart and stubs','For a lossless shorted stub Zᵢₙ=jZ₀tanβl, and for an open stub Zᵢₙ=−jZ₀cotβl. At a quarter wavelength a line transforms a resistive load Rᴸ to Z₀²/Rᴸ. The Smith chart maps normalized impedance or admittance to reflection coefficient. Move along a constant-|Γ| circle to translate along a lossless line, then use a stub to cancel the residual reactance or susceptance.','To match a 100 Ω load to a 50 Ω feed with a quarter-wave section, choose section impedance √(50·100)≈70.71 Ω. Its input is 70.71²/100≈50 Ω at the design frequency.','What impedance does an ideal short look like through a lossless quarter-wave line?','An open circuit',['A short circuit','The characteristic impedance','A fixed 50 Ω resistor'],'Quarter-wave impedance inversion turns zero load impedance into infinite input impedance ideally.'),
t('Waveguides, TE/TM modes, cutoff and cavity resonators','A hollow guide supports TE modes with zero longitudinal electric field and TM modes with zero longitudinal magnetic field. Fields propagate only above the mode cutoff. In a rectangular guide, f꜀=(v/2)√[(m/a)²+(n/b)²], and TE₁₀ is dominant when a>b. Circular guides use Bessel-function boundary conditions. An optical fiber guides light in a higher-index core surrounded by lower-index cladding.','An air-filled rectangular guide with broad dimension 2 cm has TE₁₀ cutoff c/(2a)=7.5 GHz. A 6 GHz TE₁₀ field is evanescent, while 10 GHz is above cutoff.','What is the usual dominant mode of a rectangular hollow waveguide with unequal sides?','TE₁₀',['TM₁₀','TEM','TE₀₀'],'It has the lowest cutoff for broad-side dimension a greater than b.'),
t('S-parameters, dB, dBm, insertion loss and RF blocks','S-parameters express outgoing waves in terms of incident waves at defined reference impedances. With the other port terminated in its reference impedance, S₁₁ is input reflection and S₂₁ is forward transmission. Use 20log₁₀ of amplitude ratios or 10log₁₀ of power ratios. dBm is absolute power relative to 1 mW. Insertion loss for a matched passive two-port is −20log₁₀|S₂₁|.','For |S₂₁|=0.5 and matched ports, transmitted power is 0.25 of incident power, so insertion loss is about 6.02 dB. An input of 10 dBm produces about 3.98 dBm.','What does S11 describe?','Input reflection with other ports matched',['Forward transmission','Absolute input power in dBm','Output reflection only'],'The first index is the outgoing port and the second is the excited input port.')
],[['S11','Input reflection'],['VSWR of 1','Matched line'],['Quarter-wave transformer','Impedance inversion at design frequency'],['Poynting vector','Electromagnetic power-flow density']]),

s('antennas','Antennas, Propagation, and Radar',[
t('Dipoles, monopoles, radiation patterns and beamwidth','A half-wave dipole has a current maximum at its center and radiates strongest broadside, with a null along its axis. A quarter-wave monopole over an ideal conducting plane uses its image to reproduce the upper-half dipole field. Radiation pattern gives gain versus direction. Half-power beamwidth is the angle between the two points in the main lobe where power is half its peak.','If a dipole pattern cut reaches half power at 51° and 129° measured from its axis, its half-power beamwidth is 129°−51°=78°.','Where does an ideal straight half-wave dipole have radiation nulls?','Along its axis',['All broadside directions','Only at 45°','It has no nulls'],'Current along the wire produces no far-field radiation in the axial directions.'),
t('Radiation resistance, directivity, gain, efficiency and aperture','Radiation resistance represents radiated power through I²R using RMS feed current. Loss resistance represents dissipated power. Radiation efficiency is radiated power divided by accepted power. Directivity measures peak concentration compared with an isotropic radiator at equal radiated power, and gain multiplies directivity by radiation efficiency. For a matched receiving antenna, effective aperture is λ²G/(4π).','An antenna with 60 Ω radiation resistance and 15 Ω loss resistance has efficiency 60/75=0.8. If directivity is 2.5, gain is 2.0, or about 3.01 dBi.','An antenna has directivity 4 (linear) and radiation efficiency 50%. What is gain?','2 (linear)',['4 (linear)','8 (linear)','0.5 (linear)'],'Multiply directivity by radiation efficiency.'),
t('Antenna arrays, array factor and beam steering','Fields from array elements add as complex amplitudes. For a uniform linear array, adjacent elements accumulate phase ψ=kd cosθ+β, where θ is measured from the array axis and β is the feed phase progression. Their array factor sums eʲⁿψ across elements. A main beam occurs when the phase terms align, and additional solutions can produce grating lobes. Multiply by the element pattern for the full pattern.','With spacing d=λ/2 and β=−π/2, ψ=πcosθ−π/2. Setting ψ=0 gives cosθ=0.5, so the array factor peaks at θ=60°.','What is adjusted for electronic beam steering in a phased array?','Relative element phases',['The speed of light','Only the output display','The free-space permittivity'],'A controlled phase progression changes where the emitted fields reinforce.'),
t('Ground wave, sky wave, line of sight and propagation mechanisms','Ground waves follow the surface and tend to be more useful at lower frequencies. Sky waves can return toward Earth via ionospheric refraction under suitable conditions. Line-of-sight links use a direct path. Reflection redirects waves at interfaces, diffraction bends or spreads them around edges, and scattering redistributes energy from rough surfaces or small objects.','A ridge can reduce the direct path but still allow some energy into its shadow through diffraction.','Which mechanism is associated with propagation around an edge into a shadow?','Diffraction',['Only specular reflection','Refraction in the ionosphere only','Ohmic conduction'],'Edges produce diffracted fields even where simple geometric optics predicts no direct path.'),
t('Friis equation, free-space path loss and link budget','In matched far-field free-space conditions, Pr=PtGtGr[λ/(4πd)]² using linear gains. Free-space path loss is the reciprocal of the bracketed squared factor. In a dB link budget, add transmit power in dBm and both antenna gains, then subtract propagation and other losses in dB.','Doubling the free-space separation at fixed frequency reduces receive power by about 6 dB.','Which change causes a 6 dB increase in free-space path loss, other terms fixed?','Doubling the distance',['Halving the distance','Keeping distance fixed and halving frequency','Doubling transmit power'],'Free-space path loss grows with the square of distance.'),
t('Radar blocks, pulse and CW radar, echo delay and Doppler','A basic radar has a signal source/transmitter, antenna, receiver and signal processor. A simple pulse radar estimates range from two-way travel time, R=cΔt/2. An unmodulated CW radar can measure radial velocity with Doppler but gives no ordinary absolute range measurement by delay. For monostatic sensing, Doppler magnitude is 2|vr|/λ.','A 2 µs round-trip echo corresponds to about 300 m in air.','Why is echo delay multiplied by c/2 to find range?','The echo travels to the object and back',['Radio travels at half the speed of light','One pulse contains two carrier frequencies','A delayed echo has half the transmit power'],'The measured delay is for a path of length twice the one-way range.'),
t('Pulse width, PRF, PRI, range resolution and radar equation','PRI is the time between pulses and PRF=1/PRI. The simple unambiguous-range limit is c/(2PRF) for regularly spaced pulses. For an uncompressed rectangular pulse, range resolution is roughly cτ/2; more generally bandwidth B gives roughly c/(2B). The ideal monostatic radar equation has received echo power proportional to the fourth inverse power of range because energy spreads on the outward and return paths.','An uncompressed 1 µs pulse corresponds to roughly 150 m range resolution.','What happens to simple maximum unambiguous range when PRF doubles?','It halves',['It doubles','It stays fixed','It quadruples'],'The interval allowed before the next pulse is halved.')
],[['Beamwidth','Angle between main-lobe half-power points'],['Polarization mismatch','Power factor from relative polarization'],['Pulse radar range','Computed from round-trip echo delay'],['PRF','Number of pulses transmitted per second']]),

s('embedded','Microprocessors, Microcontrollers, and Embedded Systems',[
t('CPU, ALU, registers, memory and the instruction cycle','The processor’s datapath moves values among registers, the ALU and memory. The control unit interprets the opcode and selects who reads, computes and writes at each step. The program counter identifies the next instruction. Pipelining overlaps execution stages across instructions, but dependencies, shared hardware and branches can introduce stalls or flushes.','A five-stage pipeline with one cycle per stage and no hazards takes 5+9=14 cycles for 10 instructions. The corresponding nonpipelined execution takes 50 cycles.','Which register identifies the next instruction to fetch in a basic CPU model?','Program counter',['Stack pointer','Data accumulator only','Status register'],'The program counter tracks instruction addresses.'),
t('Instruction sets, addressing modes, stack, subroutines and interrupts','An instruction set specifies operations and how operands are selected. Immediate addressing encodes the operand value in the instruction. Register addressing uses a named register. Indirect addressing uses a register as an address, and indexed addressing adds a displacement. A call records a return address, and an interrupt transfers execution to a handler while preserving the needed context.','If R2=1000, a load from 12(R2) reads memory address 1012. An instruction using immediate 12 instead uses the number 12 itself.','In immediate addressing, where is the operand encoded?','In the instruction itself',['Only in external I/O','Always at the stack top','At a separate memory address in every case'],'The immediate field directly represents the value used by the instruction.'),
t('8085, 8086, 8051 and RISC versus CISC','The 8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor with a 16-bit address bus. The 8086 has a 16-bit data path and uses segment plus offset addressing for its 20-bit address space. The 8051 is an 8-bit microcontroller family with on-chip peripheral functions. RISC often emphasizes regular encodings and load/store operations; CISC typically supports richer instruction forms.','An 8085 with 16 address bits can select 2¹⁶ byte locations, or 64 KiB.','Which classic device is a microcontroller with on-chip peripheral functions such as timers and I/O ports?','8051',['8085','8086','8087'],'The 8051 integrates a processor core with microcontroller peripherals.'),
t('Memory mapping, memory-mapped I/O and isolated I/O','An address map assigns ranges to memory and devices using address decoding. Memory-mapped I/O gives device registers ordinary memory addresses and uses normal load/store operations. Isolated I/O uses a distinct port-address space and typically dedicated access instructions. Device registers can have read or write side effects.','Writing a GPIO output register at a documented memory address can change external pin levels.','Which I/O scheme uses the same address space as memory?','Memory-mapped I/O',['Isolated I/O','DMA in all cases','Serial I/O only'],'Device registers appear in the ordinary memory address map.'),
t('Timers, counters, GPIO, PWM and peripheral interfacing','A timer counts internal clock ticks, often with a prescaler. A counter can count external events. GPIO pins can read or drive digital levels when configured correctly. PWM changes the fraction of a cycle spent high, with duty cycle Ton/T. Interfacing requires compatible voltage, drive, direction and timing.','At a 1 ms PWM period, a 250 µs high pulse gives 25% duty cycle.','What duty cycle results from a 2 ms high interval in an 8 ms period?','25%',['16%','50%','75%'],'Duty cycle equals high time divided by total period.'),
t('UART, SPI, I²C, polling, interrupts and DMA','UART uses asynchronous start/stop framing and an agreed baud rate. SPI uses a shared clock, data signals and device select. I²C uses addressed devices on two shared open-drain lines with pull-ups. Polling checks status repeatedly, interrupts notify the CPU of events, and DMA transfers blocks with limited CPU involvement.','An I²C sensor and EEPROM can share SDA and SCL if their addresses differ and the bus meets electrical limits.','Which interface normally uses addressed devices and two shared open-drain signal lines?','I²C',['UART','SPI','Parallel GPIO'],'I²C uses SDA and SCL with pull-ups and device addressing.')
],[['Program counter','Tracks instruction addresses'],['UART','Asynchronous framed serial link'],['DMA','Transfers data blocks with limited CPU intervention'],['Memory-mapped I/O','Uses addresses in the memory space']]),

s('measure','Measurements and Instrumentation',[
t('Accuracy, precision, resolution, sensitivity and loading','Accuracy is closeness to the reference value and precision is repeatability. Resolution is the smallest displayed or detectable step. Sensitivity is output change per input change. A meter can disturb the circuit through finite input impedance. Measurement error compares the indication with a reference, and uncertainty describes the range of plausible values.','A voltmeter with input resistance comparable to the node source resistance can read low because it forms a divider.','Which term describes closely repeated readings even if they are offset from the true value?','Precision',['Accuracy','Sensitivity','Loading'],'Precision concerns repeatability, so a systematic bias can coexist with high precision.'),
t('Multimeters, oscilloscopes, generators and spectrum analyzers','A multimeter measures electrical quantities, with voltage measured in parallel and current in series through a suitable input. A CRO or DSO displays voltage over time; a DSO digitizes and can store records. A function generator provides known stimuli. A spectrum analyzer gives frequency-domain levels and can show harmonics and spurs.','To inspect pulse rise time, use a suitably fast oscilloscope. To inspect harmonic frequencies directly, use a spectrum analyzer.','Which instrument directly displays the amplitudes of spectral components versus frequency?','Spectrum analyzer',['Ordinary DC ammeter','Function generator','Wheatstone bridge'],'A spectrum analyzer resolves signal power or amplitude across frequency.'),
t('Oscilloscope amplitude, period, frequency and phase','Multiply vertical divisions by volts/division and the probe factor to find voltage. Multiply horizontal divisions by time/division to get elapsed time. Frequency is reciprocal period. For equal-frequency sinusoids, phase displacement magnitude is 360°×Δt/T, with sign determined by which waveform leads.','If one waveform crosses zero 0.5 ms before the other and their period is 2 ms, it leads by 90°.','A trace spans four horizontal divisions per cycle at 0.5 ms/div. What is frequency?','500 Hz',['250 Hz','2 kHz','50 Hz'],'The period is 2 ms, so the frequency is 1/0.002 Hz.'),
t('Wheatstone bridge and AC bridges','A Wheatstone bridge compares resistive divider voltages. The detector is zero when the arm ratios match. AC bridges extend balancing to complex impedances, so the magnitude and phase balance together. Different bridge topologies are chosen for resistance, capacitance or inductance and loss measurements.','When the left arm divider ratio is 1 kΩ/2 kΩ, a right ratio of 2 kΩ/4 kΩ produces balance in the corresponding connection.','At Wheatstone bridge balance, what does the detector measure?','Zero differential voltage',['Maximum branch voltage','Infinite resistor current','An AC phase shift of 90° automatically'],'The two divider midpoints have equal voltage.'),
t('Strain gauges, RTDs, thermistors, thermocouples and LVDTs','A strain gauge converts deformation into resistance change and is often used in a bridge. An RTD uses the resistance change of a metal with temperature. A thermistor uses a strongly temperature-dependent semiconductor resistance. A thermocouple generates a voltage from a temperature difference. An LVDT uses differential transformer coupling to sense linear displacement.','For a strain gauge, fractional resistance change equals gauge factor × strain in the linear range.','Which transducer uses differential transformer coupling to measure linear displacement?','LVDT',['Thermocouple','RTD','Strain gauge'],'Motion of its core changes the relative coupling to the two secondary windings.'),
t('Signal conditioning, instrumentation gain, grounding and shielding','Signal conditioning can amplify weak sensor signals, filter noise, level-shift or isolate signals and adapt them to converters. An instrumentation amplifier amplifies a small differential voltage amid common-mode interference while presenting high input impedance. Grounding establishes references and return paths. Shielding limits coupling, and its termination must suit the interference type and frequency.','A bridge sensor may deliver a few millivolts on a much larger common-mode level. A high-CMRR instrumentation amplifier amplifies the difference.','Which amplifier property helps reject pickup present equally on both sensor leads?','High CMRR',['Low input impedance','Large offset voltage','High output ripple'],'Common-mode rejection suppresses voltage shared by both inputs.')
],[['Accuracy','Closeness to the reference value'],['Sensitivity','Change in output per change in input'],['LVDT','Linear displacement sensor'],['Wheatstone bridge','Resistive ratio measurement at balance']])
];
